  "explains": "Invoice.settlement_reference changes from settlement_reference: str | None = None to settlement_reference: str (optional becomes required). The name settlement_reference is defined in two models (Invoice and Settlement), which is within distinct_max = 3, so it is distinctive and may be searched for in consumers' code (not in comments). Consumers import the package (import marker acme_contracts): acme/api pins acme-contracts==1.10.0 on main and acme/worker pins acme-contracts==1.9.0 on main. The change is not in any tag (v1.10.0 is the latest and has the optional field), so neither consumer has it: exposure comes on adoption for both. Code that reads the field: in acme/api, services/billing/app/services/settlement.py reference_for reads invoice.settlement_reference (the Invoice import is from acme_contracts.models.invoice); the word also appears in a comment in routers/settlements.py, which is not a read. In acme/worker, worker/tasks/settlement_sync.py reads invoice.settlement_reference. In the api, reference_for is called by summarise (same file), summarise by get_settlement_summary through the aliased module import settlement_svc, which is GET /api/billing/settlements/summary (composed /api + /billing + /settlements + /summary). The web client reaches it through SETTLEMENT_SUMMARY_URL (http edge), fetchSettlementSummary and SettlementsPage, mounted at /billing/settlements. The unit test services/billing/tests/test_settlement.py imports the settlement service and is listed as a test.",
